          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Tacitus</strong><br>\nPublius Cornelius Tacitus was born around the year 56 AD in the Roman province of Gallia Narbonensis. Although little is known about his early life, it is believed he was raised in a wealthy family with strong political connections. Tacitus pursued a public career in Rome and climbed the political ladder, becoming a senator and a consul under the emperor Nerva.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tacitus's Political Career</strong><br>\nTacitus served as a senator during a tumultuous period of Roman history, which included the reigns of the Flavian dynasty and the emperors Trajan and Hadrian. His works suggest he had a complex relationship with the imperial system, often criticizing its excesses while acknowledging the stability it brought to Rome.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Works of Tacitus</strong><br>\nTacitus is widely regarded for his historical writings, which include the \"Histories\" and the \"Annals.\" These works cover the history of Rome from the death of Augustus in 14 AD to the end of the Flavian dynasty in 96 AD. His writings are valued for their detailed account of Roman political life and insightful analysis of character and motive.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
