          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Style of Writing</strong><br>\nTacitus's writing style is noted for its conciseness and somber tone. He often used pointedly brief sentences to deliver sharp judgments on the events and personalities of his times. Despite the economy of his prose, Tacitus conveyed a deep sense of the drama unfolding within the Roman state.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tacitus on the Emperors</strong><br>\nThrough his historical narratives, Tacitus provided critical evaluations of the Roman emperors. He condemned the tyranny of rulers like Nero and the instability they brought to the empire, but he also criticized the Senate for its complicity and lack of moral fortitude in the face of imperial power.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Historical Accuracy</strong><br>\nTacitus is considered one of the greatest Roman historians, yet debates continue about the accuracy of his accounts. While praised for providing essential insight into Roman history, his work must be read with awareness of his personal biases and the polemical nature of his historical interpretation.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
