            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Tacitus's Influence</strong><br>\nThe influence of Tacitus's writings extends far beyond his lifetime and the end of the Roman empire. His work has been read and respected throughout the centuries and continues to impact modern understanding of Roman history and the discipline of historiography itself.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Perception of Tacitus</strong><br>\nIn his own era, Tacitus's reputation was that of a senator of great rhetorical skill and an accomplished orator. His contemporary Pliny the Younger praised his eloquence and wisdom, reflecting the esteem in which he was held by his peers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Tacitus</strong><br>\nTacitus's legacy lies in the enduring power of his historical critique, the vividness with which he depicted the Roman world, and the directness of his style. His works remain a principal source for the study of Roman history, particularly the early Imperial period, and continue to inspire discussion and debate among historians and students alike.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
